-- Q4515: Materiality [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Misstatements of lesser amounts than overall materiality could influence users'' decisions for those items","Every account balance must have its own materiality threshold set during planning","The auditor seeks to minimize the total audit fee by narrowing testing scope","Management formally requests a separate materiality level for selected accounts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 320, specific materiality is set when the auditor determines that particular classes of transactions, balances, or disclosures exist for which misstatements of lesser amounts than overall materiality could reasonably influence users'' economic decisions. Examples include related party transactions and executive compensation.
Wrong (B): Specific materiality is not required for every account balance. It applies only when circumstances indicate certain items warrant a lower threshold.
Wrong (C): Reducing audit fees is not a basis for establishing specific materiality. The determination is driven by user sensitivity, not cost.
Wrong (D): Specific materiality is established based on the auditor''s professional judgment, not at management''s request.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4515;

-- Q4516: Materiality [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assess whether the audit strategy and plan require revision due to the change","Only revise materiality upward, never revise it downward during fieldwork","Notify the client before making any revision to the materiality amount","Defer all materiality revisions until the final evaluation of misstatements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 320, when materiality is revised during the audit, the auditor must determine whether the overall audit strategy and audit plan need revision. A downward revision may require additional procedures; an upward revision may confirm that procedures already performed were sufficient.
Wrong (B): Materiality may be revised either upward or downward depending on new information discovered during the audit.
Wrong (C): There is no requirement to notify the client before revising materiality. This is an auditor judgment decision.
Wrong (D): Materiality should be reassessed whenever new information warrants it, not deferred to audit completion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4516;

-- Q4521: Materiality [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Set at the same amount as overall group materiality","Set higher than group materiality to allow for aggregation risk","Set lower than group materiality for the financial statements as a whole","Determined independently without any reference to the group materiality level"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 600, component materiality must be set lower than group materiality for the financial statements as a whole. This ensures that the aggregate of uncorrected and undetected misstatements across all components does not exceed group materiality.
Wrong (A): Setting component materiality equal to group materiality would risk exceeding group materiality when misstatements from multiple components are aggregated.
Wrong (B): Higher component materiality would increase the risk that aggregate misstatements across components exceed group materiality.
Wrong (D): Component materiality must be determined with reference to group materiality to maintain an appropriate relationship between them.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4521;

-- Q4523: Materiality [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the quantitative dollar effect of each identified misstatement","Only misstatements found during substantive testing, excluding controls findings","Both the size and nature of misstatements relative to affected accounts and statements","Only those misstatements that individually exceed the performance materiality threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 450, the final evaluation of uncorrected misstatements requires the auditor to consider both quantitative (size) and qualitative (nature) factors in relation to particular classes of transactions, account balances, disclosures, and the financial statements as a whole. All accumulated misstatements must be included.
Wrong (A): Qualitative factors such as the nature of the misstatement and affected line items must also be considered, not just dollar amounts.
Wrong (B): All identified misstatements must be evaluated regardless of whether they were found through substantive testing or tests of controls.
Wrong (D): All uncorrected misstatements are included in the aggregate evaluation, not only those exceeding performance materiality.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4523;

-- Q4526: Responding to Assessed Risks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assigning more experienced staff, increasing supervision, and adding unpredictability","Reducing the number of audit staff assigned to the engagement overall","Setting the materiality level to zero for all financial statement accounts","Eliminating all planned tests of controls from the audit program"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, overall responses to assessed risks at the financial statement level include assigning more experienced staff, increasing supervision, incorporating elements of unpredictability, and making general changes to the nature, timing, or extent of procedures. These broad responses address pervasive risks.
Wrong (B): Reducing staff would weaken the audit response. Pervasive risks call for more experienced personnel, not fewer resources.
Wrong (C): Setting materiality to zero is neither practical nor required by auditing standards.
Wrong (D): Eliminating tests of controls removes a key source of evidence and does not appropriately address financial statement level risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4526;

-- Q4527: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Are less extensive because the higher risk has already been identified and documented","Are more persuasive by increasing quantity, relevance, or reliability of evidence","Focus exclusively on analytical procedures applied to the affected assertions","Rely primarily on inquiry of management regarding the high-risk area"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330, when assessed risk is high, the auditor designs procedures that provide more persuasive audit evidence. This can be achieved by increasing the quantity of evidence, obtaining more relevant or reliable evidence, or both. The nature, timing, and extent should all respond to the risk level.
Wrong (A): Higher assessed risk requires more extensive procedures, not less. Identifying the risk is only the first step.
Wrong (C): Analytical procedures alone are generally insufficient for high-risk assertions. A mix of procedure types provides stronger evidence.
Wrong (D): Inquiry alone does not provide sufficient persuasive evidence for assertions assessed as high risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4527;

-- Q4528: Responding to Assessed Risks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["When the audit procedures are performed relative to the period end","The number of items or transactions selected for testing in the sample","The type and purpose of procedures such as inspection, confirmation, or recalculation","The total cost of performing each planned audit procedure"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, the nature of audit procedures refers to their type and purpose, such as inspection, observation, inquiry, external confirmation, recalculation, reperformance, or analytical procedures. Nature is one of three dimensions the auditor adjusts in response to assessed risks.
Wrong (A): When procedures are performed describes the timing of audit procedures, not their nature.
Wrong (B): The number of items tested describes the extent of audit procedures, not their nature.
Wrong (D): Cost is a practical consideration but is not a defined dimension of audit procedures under auditing standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4528;

-- Q4529: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Treat the risk identically to any other assessed risk without special consideration","Rely solely on inquiry of management regarding revenue recognition policies","Design and perform procedures specifically responsive to the significant risk identified","Issue a qualified opinion solely because a significant risk has been identified"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, significant risks require special audit consideration. The auditor should design and perform procedures specifically responsive to the risk, which may include targeted substantive procedures, tests of controls, or a combination. Routine procedures alone are insufficient.
Wrong (A): Significant risks require heightened attention beyond what is applied to ordinary assessed risks.
Wrong (B): Inquiry alone does not provide sufficient appropriate evidence for a significant risk such as revenue recognition.
Wrong (D): The existence of a significant risk does not automatically lead to a qualified opinion. The auditor responds with appropriate procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4529;

-- Q4530: Responding to Assessed Risks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The budget allocated for executing the planned audit procedures","Whether procedures are performed at an interim date or at period end","The specific type of audit procedures selected for the engagement","The number of individual items included in the audit sample"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330, the timing of audit procedures refers to when they are performed: at an interim date before period end, or at or after period end. Interim testing may be efficient but creates a risk that misstatements in the remaining period go undetected.
Wrong (A): Budget is a practical consideration for engagement management but does not define the timing dimension of audit procedures.
Wrong (C): The type of procedures selected describes the nature of procedures, not their timing.
Wrong (D): The number of items in a sample describes the extent of audit procedures, not their timing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4530;

-- Q4531: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management representations that no override of controls has occurred","Test journal entries, review estimates for bias, and evaluate unusual transactions","Only test journal entries posted by the chief financial officer","Perform override procedures only when specific fraud indicators are present"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 240 requires three mandatory procedures to address management override regardless of assessed risk: (1) testing the appropriateness of journal entries and other adjustments, (2) reviewing accounting estimates for bias, and (3) evaluating the business rationale for significant unusual transactions.
Wrong (A): Management representations alone are insufficient to address the presumed risk of override. Corroborating evidence is required.
Wrong (C): Journal entry testing must cover entries from all sources, not just those posted by a single individual.
Wrong (D): Management override is a presumed risk on every engagement. These procedures are mandatory regardless of whether fraud is suspected.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4531;

-- Q4532: Responding to Assessed Risks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The quantity of a specific procedure, such as sample size or observation count","The variety of different audit procedure types selected for testing","When the procedures are performed during the course of the audit","The geographic locations where the audit procedures are carried out"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, the extent of audit procedures refers to how much of a specific procedure is performed, such as sample size for substantive testing or the number of control observations. Greater extent is appropriate when assessed risk is higher.
Wrong (B): The variety of procedure types relates to the nature of audit procedures, not their extent.
Wrong (C): When procedures are performed relates to the timing of audit procedures, not their extent.
Wrong (D): Geographic location is a logistical factor, not a defined dimension of audit procedures under auditing standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4532;

-- Q4533: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reduce the number of inventory observations performed at client locations","Perform only inquiry and analytical procedures on inventory balances","Engage a specialist, test a larger sample, and inspect inventory at additional locations","Rely on the prior year''s inventory valuation testing without updating procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, when inventory valuation risk is assessed as high, the auditor responds with more robust procedures. This may include engaging specialists for complex valuations, increasing sample sizes for price testing, and inspecting inventory at additional locations to verify condition and existence.
Wrong (A): Reducing observations weakens audit evidence, the opposite of the appropriate response to high assessed risk.
Wrong (B): Inquiry and analytical procedures alone are insufficient for a high-risk assertion like inventory valuation.
Wrong (D): Prior-year testing does not provide evidence about current-year inventory valuation and does not address the assessed high risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4533;

-- Q4535: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reduces the overall cost of conducting the audit engagement","Makes the audit more efficient by streamlining planned procedures","Helps detect fraud by preventing management from anticipating specific procedures","Satisfies specific regulatory requirements for publicly traded entities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 240, incorporating unpredictability helps detect fraud because it prevents management and employees from anticipating which procedures will be performed, when, and on what items. Examples include surprise counts, testing unusual periods, and varying sampling methods.
Wrong (A): Unpredictability is designed to enhance fraud detection effectiveness, not to reduce audit costs.
Wrong (B): The purpose is fraud detection, not efficiency. Unpredictable procedures may actually require additional effort to plan and execute.
Wrong (D): Unpredictability is a professional judgment response to fraud risk under AU-C 240, not a specific regulatory mandate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4535;

-- Q4536: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is likely to result in a qualified opinion on the financial statements","Requires special audit consideration due to its nature or magnitude of potential misstatement","Automatically requires the auditor to issue a disclaimer of opinion","Cannot be adequately addressed by any audit procedure available to the auditor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, a significant risk is an identified risk of material misstatement that requires special audit consideration in the auditor''s professional judgment. Factors include complexity, subjectivity, susceptibility to fraud, and the degree of estimation uncertainty.
Wrong (A): A significant risk does not automatically lead to a qualified opinion. The auditor designs responsive procedures to address the risk.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer is reserved for scope limitations, not for the identification of significant risks that can be addressed through audit procedures.
Wrong (D): Significant risks can and must be addressed through specifically designed audit procedures, including substantive procedures and tests of details.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4536;

-- Q4537: Responding to Assessed Risks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Perform additional substantive procedures or combined procedures to cover the remaining period","Issue the audit report as of the interim testing date rather than year-end","Accept management representations to bridge the gap from interim to period end","Reduce the scope of year-end procedures since interim testing was already completed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, when substantive procedures are performed at an interim date, the auditor must perform additional substantive procedures, tests of controls, or a combination for the remaining period to bridge the gap to period end and obtain a reasonable basis for the opinion.
Wrong (B): The audit report date relates to when sufficient appropriate evidence has been obtained, not to when interim procedures were performed.
Wrong (C): Management representations alone do not provide sufficient appropriate evidence to cover the remaining period after interim testing.
Wrong (D): Interim testing creates an evidence gap that must be bridged with additional procedures, not addressed by reducing year-end scope.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4537;

-- Q4538: Responding to Assessed Risks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only analytical procedures applied to the revenue recognition assertions","Only tests of controls over the revenue recognition process","Substantive procedures specifically designed to address the risk, including tests of details","Only inquiry of management about the nature and extent of the risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, for significant risks, substantive procedures must be specifically responsive to the assessed risk. If the approach consists only of substantive procedures, those procedures must include tests of details because analytical procedures alone may not provide sufficiently precise evidence.
Wrong (A): Analytical procedures alone are insufficient for significant risks. Tests of details are required when the substantive approach is the sole response.
Wrong (B): Tests of controls alone do not address the need for substantive evidence specifically responsive to a significant risk.
Wrong (D): Inquiry alone does not provide sufficient appropriate audit evidence for a significant risk such as revenue recognition.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4538;

-- Q4540: Responding to Assessed Risks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Have a clear linkage between the assessed risks and the nature, timing, and extent chosen","Be identical for all identified risks regardless of their assessed level of severity","Focus only on assertions with the highest assessed risk of material misstatement","Be limited exclusively to substantive analytical procedures for all assertions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, further audit procedures must have a clear linkage between the assessed risks at the assertion level and the nature, timing, and extent of procedures designed to respond to those risks. This ensures the audit plan is specifically responsive to identified risks.
Wrong (B): Procedures should be tailored to each risk''s assessed level. A uniform approach fails to address varying risk levels appropriately.
Wrong (C): All relevant assertions require appropriate procedures, not only those assessed at the highest level of risk.
Wrong (D): The auditor selects from a full range of procedure types. Limiting to analytical procedures alone would be insufficient for many assertions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4540;

-- Q4542: Responding to Assessed Risks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A decrease in the assessed risk of material misstatement for the account","Management''s preference for minimizing disruption during interim fieldwork","A reduction in the overall audit budget allocated for the engagement","An increase in the assessed risk of material misstatement since interim testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, shifting substantive procedures from interim to period end is typically driven by an increase in assessed risk since interim testing was performed. Testing at period end provides stronger evidence because it covers the full period and eliminates the gap between interim and year-end.
Wrong (A): A decrease in assessed risk would support keeping interim testing, not shifting procedures to period end where more effort is required.
Wrong (B): Management''s scheduling preferences do not drive the auditor''s decision about the timing of substantive procedures.
Wrong (C): Budget constraints are not a valid basis for audit timing decisions. Timing is driven by risk assessment and evidence needs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4542;

-- Q4544: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only substantive procedures with no consideration of control effectiveness","Only management inquiry regarding whether controls are operating effectively","Only analytical procedures applied to the assertions where controls are expected to work","Tests of controls to obtain evidence about the operating effectiveness of those controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, when the auditor''s risk assessment includes an expectation that controls are operating effectively, the auditor must test those controls to obtain evidence about their operating effectiveness. The auditor cannot simply assume controls work without evidence.
Wrong (A): If the risk assessment relies on control effectiveness, the auditor must test those controls rather than using only substantive procedures.
Wrong (B): Inquiry of management alone does not provide sufficient evidence that controls are operating effectively.
Wrong (C): Analytical procedures test account balances and transactions, not the operating effectiveness of internal controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4544;

-- Q4545: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management''s valuation of the derivatives without performing further testing","Rely on the prior year''s accounting treatment of derivatives as sufficient evidence","Exclude the complex derivatives from the scope of the current audit engagement","Design specific substantive procedures to test derivative valuation and classification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, complex derivative transactions near year-end present significant risks related to valuation, classification, and disclosure. The auditor should design specific substantive procedures, which may include engaging a valuation specialist, testing fair value inputs and assumptions, and confirming terms with counterparties.
Wrong (A): Accepting management''s valuation without testing fails to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence for a high-risk area.
Wrong (B): Prior-year treatment does not provide evidence about current-year transactions, especially for complex instruments with changing market conditions.
Wrong (C): Excluding significant transactions from audit scope is not appropriate and would constitute a scope limitation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4545;

-- Q4547: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the specialist''s findings without any further evaluation or testing","Evaluate the specialist''s competence, objectivity, and reasonableness of findings","Require that the specialist be a direct employee of the audit firm","Disregard the specialist''s work entirely and perform all procedures independently"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 500, when management uses a specialist, the auditor should evaluate the specialist''s competence, capabilities, and objectivity. The auditor must also assess whether the specialist''s work is adequate, including the relevance and reasonableness of assumptions, methods, and findings.
Wrong (A): The auditor must evaluate the specialist''s work, not accept it without scrutiny. Uncritical acceptance does not satisfy evidence requirements.
Wrong (C): A management''s specialist need not be employed by the audit firm. The auditor evaluates the specialist regardless of employment relationship.
Wrong (D): Ignoring relevant specialist work is inefficient and unnecessary. The auditor can use it as evidence after proper evaluation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4547;

-- Q4550: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the service organization because it falls outside the client entity","Understand how the service organization''s services affect the client''s internal control","Require the client to bring all outsourced processing back in-house before audit","Issue a disclaimer of opinion because the service organization cannot be tested"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 402, when a client uses a service organization for significant transaction processing, the auditor must understand how those services affect the client''s internal control relevant to the audit. The auditor assesses risks and determines whether sufficient evidence can be obtained, potentially through a SOC report.
Wrong (A): The service organization''s processing is part of the client''s information system. Ignoring it leaves a gap in the auditor''s understanding of internal control.
Wrong (C): There is no requirement that clients perform all processing internally. The auditor must adapt procedures to the outsourced environment.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer is not automatic. The auditor can obtain evidence through SOC reports, complementary controls, or alternative procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4550;

-- Q4551: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only describes the service organization''s system design at a point in time","Includes a description plus tests of control operating effectiveness over a period","Is prepared by the user organization rather than the service organization''s auditor","Does not require involvement of an independent auditor for its preparation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C 320, a Type 2 SOC 1 report includes a description of the service organization''s system and tests of the operating effectiveness of controls over a specified period, typically six to twelve months. This provides more useful evidence than a Type 1 report for the user auditor.
Wrong (A): A Type 1 report covers only design suitability at a point in time. The Type 2 goes further by testing operating effectiveness over a period.
Wrong (C): Both Type 1 and Type 2 reports are prepared by the service auditor engaged by the service organization, not by the user organization.
Wrong (D): Both report types require an independent service auditor to examine and report on the service organization''s controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4551;

-- Q4552: Using the Work of Others [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Replace all substantive testing the external auditor would otherwise perform","Use their completed work as evidence or have them provide direct assistance","Eliminate the external auditor''s need to understand the client''s internal control","Allow the internal auditors to sign and issue the independent audit report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 610, the external auditor may use the internal audit function in two ways: (1) using work already performed by internal auditors as audit evidence, or (2) requesting direct assistance under the external auditor''s direction and supervision. The external auditor retains sole responsibility for the opinion.
Wrong (A): Internal audit work cannot replace all substantive testing. The external auditor must still perform procedures on significant risks and judgmental areas.
Wrong (C): The external auditor must always obtain an understanding of internal control regardless of whether internal audit work is used.
Wrong (D): Only the external auditor can sign and issue the independent audit report. Internal auditors cannot assume that responsibility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4552;

-- Q4553: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether internal audit reports to governance and is free from operational responsibilities","The compensation levels and bonus structure of the internal audit staff","Whether each internal auditor holds an active CPA license in their state","The total number of years the internal audit department has been in existence"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 610, objectivity is primarily assessed by examining the internal audit function''s organizational status and reporting relationships. An internal audit function that reports to those charged with governance and is free from operational responsibilities is more likely to be objective.
Wrong (B): Compensation levels are not a primary factor in evaluating internal audit objectivity. Reporting structure and organizational independence are the key considerations.
Wrong (C): CPA licensure relates to competence, not objectivity. Internal auditors are not required to hold CPA licenses to perform their function.
Wrong (D): The department''s longevity does not directly indicate whether the internal audit function is objective in its current operations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4553;

-- Q4554: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Increase direct testing because heavy reliance on internal audit work is inappropriate here","Accept the internal auditors'' conclusions on those areas without additional testing","Reduce the scope of external audit procedures in those high-judgment areas","Delegate the significant judgment determinations entirely to the internal audit function"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 610, in areas involving significant judgments such as accounting estimates or going concern evaluations, the external auditor should limit reliance on internal audit work and increase the extent of direct testing. Greater judgment requires greater external auditor involvement.
Wrong (B): Accepting conclusions without testing is inappropriate for areas requiring significant judgment, where the external auditor''s own assessment is essential.
Wrong (C): Reducing scope in high-judgment areas would weaken audit evidence precisely where stronger evidence is needed.
Wrong (D): Significant judgments cannot be delegated to internal auditors. The external auditor must exercise independent professional judgment on these matters.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4554;

-- Q4555: Using the Work of Others [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is engaged or employed by the auditor to help obtain sufficient appropriate evidence","Is always required to hold a CPA license in the relevant jurisdiction","Must be a full-time employee of the audit firm rather than an outside contractor","Cannot provide audit evidence related to accounting estimates or valuations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 620, an auditor''s specialist is an individual or organization with expertise in a field other than accounting or auditing, whose work is used by the auditor to assist in obtaining sufficient appropriate audit evidence. The specialist may be engaged externally or employed internally.
Wrong (B): An auditor''s specialist is not required to be a CPA. They provide expertise in non-accounting fields such as engineering, geology, or valuation.
Wrong (C): The specialist may be either an employee of the firm or an external contractor. There is no requirement for full-time employment.
Wrong (D): Specialists frequently provide evidence about accounting estimates, fair value measurements, and other complex valuations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4555;

-- Q4556: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Perform only analytical procedures on the significant component''s financial information","Rely solely on the component auditor''s report without further group-level review","Perform or have the component auditor perform an audit using component materiality","Exclude the financially significant component from the consolidated group audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 600, for a component that is individually financially significant to the group, the group engagement team or a component auditor should perform an audit of the component''s financial information using component materiality. This ensures sufficient appropriate evidence for the group opinion.
Wrong (A): Analytical procedures alone are insufficient for a component that is individually significant to the group financial statements.
Wrong (B): The group engagement team must actively evaluate the component auditor''s work and communications, not simply accept the report.
Wrong (D): Excluding a significant component would create a scope limitation and undermine the basis for the group audit opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4556;

-- Q4558: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the exceptions because the service auditor has already addressed them","Automatically disclaim an opinion on the user entity''s financial statements","Evaluate whether the exceptions indicate deficiencies affecting the user entity''s statements","Require the service organization to reissue the report with all exceptions removed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 402, when a SOC 1 Type 2 report contains exceptions, the user auditor should evaluate whether those exceptions affect controls relevant to the user entity''s financial statements. The auditor considers the nature and significance of exceptions, complementary user controls, and whether additional procedures are needed.
Wrong (A): The user auditor cannot ignore exceptions. Each must be assessed for its potential effect on the user entity''s financial statement assertions.
Wrong (B): Exceptions in a SOC report do not automatically warrant a disclaimer. The auditor evaluates their significance and may perform compensating procedures.
Wrong (D): The user auditor has no authority to require the service organization to reissue its report. The auditor must assess and respond to the exceptions as reported.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4558;

-- Q4559: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Review component auditor communications, findings, and evaluate evidence sufficiency","Simply accept each component auditor''s report without any further review","Visit each component location and personally supervise all audit testing","Only review the component auditors'' billing statements and fee arrangements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 600, the group engagement partner must review component auditor communications including findings, significant risks, and control deficiencies. The partner must evaluate whether sufficient appropriate evidence has been obtained to support the group audit opinion.
Wrong (B): Simply accepting component reports without review fails to satisfy the group engagement partner''s responsibility for the overall audit.
Wrong (C): Personal visits to every component are not required. The partner fulfills oversight through communication review, evaluation, and direction.
Wrong (D): Billing statements provide no information about audit quality or the sufficiency of evidence obtained at the component level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4559;
